WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:18.000
Welcome to this presentation. Hi, I'm Thomas and today I will speak about

00:18.000 --> 00:26.000
Secure Boot in Embedded Linux systems. But first, I am going to introduce myself.

00:26.000 --> 00:34.000
I am an embedded Linux engineer and a member of the bootlim team since few months.

00:34.000 --> 00:41.000
Moreover, I am also a contributor to open source projects.

00:41.000 --> 00:46.000
I am living in France, close to Toulouse.

00:46.000 --> 00:50.000
This presentation is divided in two parts.

00:50.000 --> 00:59.000
The first part introduces Secure Boot as well as some notion about signature process.

00:59.000 --> 01:07.000
I will also speak about the impact that I can bring.

01:07.000 --> 01:17.000
The second part is an example of Secure Boot implementation on IMX 8 platform

01:17.000 --> 01:26.000
which has the particularity of not requiring an initial MFS.

01:29.000 --> 01:42.000
Thickerboot is a feature to verify the parts used to boot a system, including hardware and software,

01:42.000 --> 01:58.000
to protect the system against hijacking, to protect users against tampering applications, hardware fault and malware execution.

01:58.000 --> 02:08.000
Finally, that allows the vendor to meet legal and commercial goals.

02:08.000 --> 02:19.840
Another name for Seeker Boot is Chain of Trust because the main functionality of this feature

02:19.840 --> 02:32.000
is Hat Boot. Each stage is able to verify the integrity of the next stage before its execution.

02:32.000 --> 02:49.240
For it, each stage contains a signature created a build that represents its integrity, as

02:49.240 --> 02:59.440
well as the public key of the next stage in order to perform the integrity checking.

02:59.440 --> 03:13.440
Here, I am introducing the notion of asymmetric signature used in the chain of trust.

03:13.440 --> 03:22.920
Asymmetric signature is a couple of keys, the public keys that will be shared with recipients

03:22.920 --> 03:33.960
to perform the data integrity checking and the private key that must be secret is used

03:33.960 --> 03:41.240
by the issuer to send shared data.

03:41.240 --> 03:50.960
The data is hashed then signed to be shared with recipients in addition to the public

03:50.960 --> 04:02.400
key. Then recipients are able to verify that the digest of the received data is correct.

04:05.600 --> 04:11.680
Enable secure boot also involves to be able

04:11.680 --> 04:25.600
to manage keys, especially to be able to protect private keys, to lock devices during the manufacturing,

04:26.880 --> 04:32.560
to boot upgrade images including signature and public keys,

04:32.560 --> 04:43.660
and to accept a raise of the boot time duration because it had some crypto

04:43.660 --> 04:54.340
operation to perform the checking. Here I will speak about an example of secure

04:54.340 --> 05:10.140
boot implementation done for a target based on an EMX8. So secure boot can be implemented

05:10.140 --> 05:21.140
in several ways, depending on requirements. In this example, there are four main parts

05:21.140 --> 05:30.740
according to the boot process of the target. To check the bootloader integrity there is only one

05:30.740 --> 05:40.820
choice that depends on the silicon vendor. Here the feature is provided by NXP and called

05:40.820 --> 05:53.540
advanced height assurance boot. The kernel checking, we show to use an hubboot

05:53.540 --> 06:05.180
feature named VerifiedBoot. And for the rootFS part, we choose to use DMVerity

06:05.180 --> 06:10.940
because it is simple and it is able to use hardware offloading.

06:14.700 --> 06:22.620
The advanced high high surveillance boot is more complex than the secure boot feature

06:22.620 --> 06:33.020
implemented for previous IMX platform. As for the high high surveillance boot

06:33.020 --> 06:42.440
of previous EMX platform. It fuses asymmetric keys and one-time program app fuses to store

06:42.440 --> 06:55.520
hatches of public keys. Bootloader stages our packages into a signed image through code signing

06:55.520 --> 07:07.160
tool. And the secure statue can be checked from Herboot with the hub statue command.

07:07.160 --> 07:23.000
But its boot flow is very different because it used a security controller and it's also

07:23.000 --> 07:37.760
able to start a binary for a SP processor. Then it uses two containers.

07:40.640 --> 07:50.900
The images contain some binaries. It is divided in two main parts. The first

07:50.900 --> 08:02.900
The first contains the binaries of security controller firmware and its signature using NXP keys.

08:02.900 --> 08:19.900
The second contains the SPL, the harm tested firmware, hubboot, and the associated signature using hohohem keys.

08:19.900 --> 08:36.900
At boot, the system controller unit loads the signature controller firmware, then others bootcoder stage.

08:36.900 --> 08:44.900
And at each step, the security controller validates the binary.

08:44.900 --> 09:04.900
When all binaries have been loaded and verified, they are executed each at their turn.

09:05.300 --> 09:14.180
The first step is to generate OM keys. Keys are built with a script using a file

09:14.820 --> 09:22.580
that contains trace, passphrase, and some additional parameters to configure the kind,

09:22.580 --> 09:25.860
the size, the key, and the validity period.

09:25.860 --> 09:42.780
Here we choose to use elliptic algorithm because there is a limitation in silicon B0 of IMXX8.

09:42.780 --> 09:53.580
In addition, it is necessary to generate the hash table as well as here checksum that should

09:53.580 --> 10:03.500
be stored in the one-time programmer fuses. So there is another script for this purpose.

10:05.740 --> 10:14.940
It is also possible to use the hodi command to check the hash table in order to be sure

10:14.940 --> 10:18.060
that the values burnt will be correct.

10:21.900 --> 10:31.660
Once we have the keys, the stables, the bootloader image, then it is now necessary to write a little

10:33.420 --> 10:43.340
configuration file for coding and tools in order to build the same image.

10:43.340 --> 10:57.140
Here you can see an example. It contains patches to the Hatch table, to the public key, the

10:57.140 --> 11:08.540
bootloader binary that should be assigned. But also the offset of the header signature

11:08.540 --> 11:17.720
block that's depending on the target and that can be found from the datasheet.

11:17.720 --> 11:34.100
The sign and bootloader will not be verified until the fuses are burned.

11:34.100 --> 11:51.100
So, here, it is an example of commands performed from Huboot to burn and to read them.

11:51.100 --> 12:01.100
Before and after burning fuses, it is also possible from Huboot to check the secure boot status.

12:01.100 --> 12:17.100
In case of issue, the SecureController returns an event that can be print with the HAB Statute command.

12:19.100 --> 12:25.100
The event code returned corresponds to a kind of issue.

12:25.100 --> 12:32.100
In addition, events are erased by container.

12:32.100 --> 12:43.100
Finally, if no event occurs, then the targets can be locked

12:43.100 --> 12:56.100
Thanks to the command HAB clause that will burn and additional fuse to prevent unsigned boot loader execution.

12:56.100 --> 13:01.100
Let's move to the kernel signature.

13:01.100 --> 13:07.100
Here we choose to use a boot feature called verified boot.

13:07.100 --> 13:12.100
This feature uses fitimage.

13:12.100 --> 13:23.100
FitImage is a container and which content is described by an image source file.

13:23.100 --> 13:36.100
Basically, an image source file is a list of nodes representing each element of the FitImage.

13:36.100 --> 13:48.100
It also uses asymmetric keys with which public key will be stored in a boot binary and the

13:48.100 --> 13:55.100
private key is used to sign the kernel image had built.

13:55.100 --> 14:05.100
The kernel image is built and signed in three steps through the DTC and the make image command.

14:06.100 --> 14:16.100
The build is ensigned. The first step is to build the ensigned kernel image.

14:16.100 --> 14:21.100
The second step is to build the signed kernel image.

14:21.100 --> 14:29.100
And finally, the last step is to inject the public key in HuBoot.

14:29.100 --> 14:40.220
FitImage can contain some images and configurations.

14:40.220 --> 14:50.940
Images can be a kernel image, a device tree binary, a device tree overlay, a boot script,

14:50.940 --> 14:54.940
and so on.

14:54.940 --> 15:07.740
configuration is a combination of these images. From this example, the fit image contains only one

15:07.740 --> 15:21.980
kernel image, only one kernel device tribunary and only one reboot script, as well as only one

15:21.980 --> 15:28.540
configuration using a combination of all the provided images.

15:31.660 --> 15:38.860
Moreover, images and configuration from the fit image are asked.

15:44.140 --> 15:46.060
There is two ways to send a fit image.

15:46.060 --> 15:57.340
either send individually each image, either send each available configuration.

15:59.740 --> 16:07.260
Then a configuration allows to prevent mix and max attack.

16:07.260 --> 16:24.420
This example uses a second way, then a signature node has been added in the configuration node.

16:24.420 --> 16:39.420
node. If we have chosen to sign individually each image, then a signature node would have

16:39.420 --> 16:52.340
been added in each node describing an image. The following commands allow to create a key

16:52.340 --> 16:59.140
as well as a public key and a certificate that will be used to sign the kernel.

17:02.020 --> 17:10.500
Now, this is an example of command that will be used to build and sign the kernel

17:10.500 --> 17:16.980
and allow to inject the public key into the root binary.

17:16.980 --> 17:25.980
Now, how it meant to care the root interface and take a deep checking.

17:25.980 --> 17:31.980
Here, there are some available solutions.

17:31.980 --> 17:37.980
And we choose to use DMVivity.

17:37.980 --> 17:51.180
Basically, Jam Verity is a virtual file system using a hash tree where each leaf is a hash

17:51.180 --> 18:02.860
of a block of the file system, then Jam Verity can be only used with block devices like squash

18:02.860 --> 18:17.300
and so on. In addition, it can be only used for read-only devices. By default, the hash

18:17.300 --> 18:30.860
tree is stored into a separate block device called hashDevice, but it is also possible

18:30.860 --> 18:43.340
to concatenate it with the data device. In addition, it is also possible to sign the route fsh.

18:50.060 --> 18:59.420
Following a few commands to create a DMVRT device, then to check it and to open it.

19:00.860 --> 19:05.860
As you can see, this is a very hazy.

19:05.860 --> 19:10.860
Here the hash tree is append at the end of the data image.

19:10.860 --> 19:21.860
Then we use an additional parameter, hash offset, to specify the end of the data.

19:21.860 --> 19:32.860
In addition, the integrity can be checked with you knowing the root hash, but it is necessary to want it.

19:36.860 --> 19:45.860
It is not the case in this example, but it is also possible to sign the root afese hash

19:45.860 --> 19:53.860
with the key that will be stored in the kernel keyring.

19:53.860 --> 20:04.860
Finally, one of the requirements of the project was

20:04.860 --> 20:11.860
we don't need to use a in-itram FS to prepare the real-root FS device.

20:11.860 --> 20:21.860
To do that, we use dm init that allows from the kernel command line to early mount

20:22.740 --> 20:27.620
dm-verity device that contains the rootfs file system.

20:27.620 --> 20:40.580
and in addition we chose to use a boot script stored in the fit image and loaded

20:40.580 --> 20:48.820
by uboot between the command that load the kernel and the one that boot it.

20:48.820 --> 21:03.820
As this boot script is a part of the fit image, then it is also assigned.

21:03.820 --> 21:13.180
script is useful to store parameters that are required by the adminit to early mount the

21:13.180 --> 21:28.460
DUMVAS MIPER like the root fsh, the block size, so on. Thank you for attending this presentation.

21:29.020 --> 21:30.460
I hope you like it.

